@QuantVector Insane fallacy. Why are you talking about my returns instead of addressing any of the points I actually made? What do my gains have anything to do with whether my argument about AI economics and circular financing is correct?

If you can't address the actual points, then attacking me personally because you assume I'm underperforming the market adds absolutely nothing to the discussion. I could have 10x'd the market or lost 90% and the argument would still be exactly the same.

If you have an actual counterargument to the points I made, make it. If you don't, you should probably stay quiet because telling me to short it, stay in cash or that I'm “missing out” doesn't address a single thing I said.

@kylekookie1440 I honestly don't know how you got my actual positioning from my comment. I'm always invested, regardless of the market environment. That's not really up for debate. We also know historically that missing just a handful of the strongest green days can have a huge impact on long-term returns, so I'm certainly not sitting in cash waiting for some perfect crash.

What I'm actually doing is trimming the positions that are most exposed to the two issues I mentioned, while always keeping some cash on the side to buy dips. If a stock gets hit but I still believe the underlying business is strong and that it will recover, that's exactly where I want to deploy that cash.

So no, I'm not sitting here waiting for the apocalypse or trying to time the market. I'm managing my exposure to risks I think are real while staying invested and taking advantage of opportunities when they arise. That's quite different from being a “perma-bear” sitting in cash for years waiting for a crash.
